10:32:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: “1080p” only describes the **resolution** (1920×1080). It does not specify how much compression, frame rate, color quality, or bitrate is used. Stadia can use more data than a typical 1080p YouTube video because: - **Higher frame rate:** Stadia commonly streams at **60 fps**, while much YouTube content is 24–30 fps. Twice as many frames can mean substantially more data. - **Games are difficult to compress:** Fast camera movement, detailed grass/foliage, particles, HUD elements, and constantly changing scenes give video compression less opportunity to reuse information from prior frames. - **Low latency matters:** YouTube can buffer far ahead and use encoding strategies that prioritize squeezing the file size down. A cloud game needs to respond to your controller input almost immediately, so it uses real-time encoding settings that may be less bandwidth-efficient. - **Quality targets differ:** Stadia needs text, UI, and rapid movement to remain readable/playable. Aggressive compression artifacts can be more disruptive in a game than in a passive video. - **Codec/settings may differ:** YouTube often serves very efficient codecs such as VP9 or AV1, especially for popular videos, and dynamically lowers bitrate when appropriate. Stadia’s stream quality depends on the device, connection, codec support, and selected mode. For example, Stadia’s 1080p/60 mode was often quoted around **10–12.6 GB per hour** (roughly 22–28 Mbps). A typical 1080p YouTube stream may be only around **3–6 GB per hour**, especially at 30 fps—though high-quality 1080p60 YouTube can use more. So it is not that Stadia’s pixels are “more 1080p”; it is sending a more demanding, real-time 60-fps interactive video stream at a higher bitrate.
