10:32:59 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=501
10:32:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not necessarily. They have the same **number of pixels**, but not necessarily the same **picture quality**. Think of 1080p as the size of a canvas: both images have 1,920 × 1,080 pixels. But one can be saved with much heavier compression than the other. At the same 1080p resolution, differences can include: - **Bitrate:** More bits per second preserves more image detail. Low bitrate causes blockiness, smearing, banding, and muddy motion. - **Frame rate:** 1080p30 and 1080p60 are both 1080p, but 60 fps has twice as many images each second and smoother motion. - **Compression codec:** H.264, VP9, AV1, etc. can produce different quality at the same bitrate. - **Color quality:** Streams can differ in color depth, chroma subsampling, HDR support, and color range. - **Motion/detail:** A static talking-head video can look excellent at a low bitrate; a fast-moving game needs far more data to look comparably clean. So two things can both be “1080p” while one looks noticeably worse or better. Resolution tells you **how many pixels are displayed**, not how accurately each pixel’s color and detail survive compression.
