10:33:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: 1080p guarantees only one thing: the displayed frame has **1920 × 1080 pixel positions**. It does **not** guarantee that the information in those pixels is equally accurate. Compression changes the pixel values before they reach your TV. For example, two 1080p streams can both fill every one of the same 2,073,600 pixel locations: - Stream A: each frame is heavily compressed, so fine textures become blurry and moving areas smear. - Stream B: more data is used, so those same pixels retain more distinct detail and cleaner motion. Both are still literally 1080p. The resolution label remains correct. If they look the same to you on your TV, at your distance, with that content, then the extra quality is not perceptible or important in your use case—and that is completely reasonable. But that does not make the streams technically identical, or make bitrate irrelevant to what is encoded in those pixels.
